00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:04.756
And what we build can be shared with other targeted users
for testing and iteratively improved upon all.

00:25:04.756 --> 00:25:07.076
Within SageMaker Unified Studio,

00:25:08.796 --> 00:25:12.116
the capability to build and test agents is the bridge

00:25:12.116 --> 00:25:20.156
to our third phase the legal research agent.
Let me quickly recap where we are.

00:25:21.596 --> 00:25:29.156
We have a production legal search on launch.com, a conversational
Q&A over judgments and legislation with responsible usage controls.

00:25:30.076 --> 00:25:35.396
We then use data available from the usage of the system
to see what areas could be improved upon.

00:25:36.356 --> 00:25:44.556
This involves analyzing the top areas of law, intents of legal
research, and experimenting on knowledge bases as inputs to an agent.

00:25:44.716 --> 00:25:49.516
And we were convinced and have enough evidence
to back building a system that conducts legal research

00:25:49.516 --> 00:25:55.116
the way a junior lawyer would searching,
reading, summarizing and synthesizing across multiple sources.

00:25:55.716 --> 00:25:59.156
Which brings us from legal search to legal research.

00:25:59.636 --> 00:26:02.156
And in the form of a legal research agent.

00:26:07.116 --> 00:26:11.556
This legal research agent is a conversational
AI system powered by Agentic AI.

00:26:11.716 --> 00:26:13.916
And this is where everything comes together.

00:26:15.236 --> 00:26:18.436
The key innovation is that it doesn't reinvent the wheel.

00:26:18.436 --> 00:26:21.476
Instead, it orchestrates existing products.

00:26:21.916 --> 00:26:24.316
The legal search that Alvaro talked about earlier,

00:26:24.316 --> 00:26:29.396
our summarization service and our legal
principle extraction tool all as agent tools.

00:26:30.556 --> 00:26:36.756
The agent decides which tools to use and in what order based
on the user's research question.

00:26:37.076 --> 00:26:45.956
For example, a lawyer might ask, find me recent cases on breach
of fiduciary duty in the context of company directors

00:26:45.956 --> 00:26:53.476
and summarize the key principles the agent would first search
for relevant cases, summarize each one,

00:26:53.996 --> 00:26:57.516
then extract and synthesize the legal principles across all of them.

00:26:59.156 --> 00:27:03.396
Lawnet has built a proof of concept and is currently
reviewing evaluation frameworks

00:27:03.396 --> 00:27:06.836
and agent workflows before opening it up for business user testing.

00:27:08.316 --> 00:27:10.236
The value proposition is composable.

00:27:10.236 --> 00:27:17.916
AI. Build each capability once, test it thoroughly,
and then reuse it as a tool in an agent framework.

00:27:18.036 --> 00:27:21.836
This dramatically accelerates development and ensures quality.

00:27:25.396 --> 00:27:27.916
Here's what the research agent looks like in practice.

00:27:27.916 --> 00:27:30.636
The agent introduces itself and explains what it can do.

00:27:30.636 --> 00:27:35.676
Search cases within a repository generate clear headnotes,
summarizing the key legal principles,

00:27:35.676 --> 00:27:39.156
and help lawyers focus on analysis rather than manual review.

00:27:39.156 --> 00:27:43.676
Earlier, you saw the quick action buttons at the bottom.
Q and A search and summarization,

00:27:43.676 --> 00:27:47.036
which lets users invoke specific tools directly if they want to.

00:27:47.876 --> 00:27:54.916
But the real power is in the free form conversation, where
the agent autonomously decides which tools to use and in what order.

00:27:56.276 --> 00:27:59.076
The design philosophy is to augment, not to replace.

00:27:59.116 --> 00:28:02.236
We want lawyers spending their time on legal reasoning and strategy,

00:28:02.236 --> 00:28:06.476
not on the mechanical work of finding
and reading through tons of documents.

00:28:11.116 --> 00:28:15.036
Let me walk you through the technical
architecture of the research agent.

00:28:15.836 --> 00:28:18.636
On the left, we have the client a react application.

00:28:19.596 --> 00:28:25.276
This communicates with Amazon API gateway,
which handles authentication via Amazon Cognito.

00:28:25.676 --> 00:28:31.956
We use server sent events streaming so that the user
can see the agent's thinking and responses in real time.

00:28:33.876 --> 00:28:40.076
The API gateway connects to the AgentCore runtime,
which hosts our agent backed by Amazon Bedrock.

00:28:40.116 --> 00:28:46.676
This is the brain. It interprets the user's requests
plans which tools to use and orchestrates the workflow.

00:28:48.956 --> 00:28:53.756
The agent communicates with the AgentCore gateway,
which hosts our MCP server.

00:28:53.756 --> 00:28:56.716
The MCP server handles tool search and tool routing.

00:28:56.716 --> 00:29:00.236
It knows which tools are available and how to invoke them.

00:29:03.076 --> 00:29:08.876
Each tool is implemented as a Lambda function behind the gateway,
and when the agent decides to perform a search,

00:29:08.876 --> 00:29:11.596
the Lambda invokes our existing search workflow.

00:29:11.636 --> 00:29:16.236
The same query preprocessing,
enhanced search, and inference pipeline we showed earlier.

00:29:17.356 --> 00:29:19.196
There are two things I want to highlight.

00:29:19.196 --> 00:29:25.516
First, in the middle of the screen filter injection,
we inject user specific filters at the gateway level,

00:29:25.516 --> 00:29:29.356
so access controls are enforced regardless of what the agent requests.

00:29:30.476 --> 00:29:33.996
Second, the reuse of existing infrastructure.

00:29:34.036 --> 00:29:42.276
The search workflow the agent uses is the exact same one that powers
the production search on lawnet.com. We didn't rebuild anything,

00:29:42.516 --> 00:29:44.796
we just made it accessible as a tool.

00:29:51.956 --> 00:29:55.556
Looking ahead, there are four key initiatives on our roadmap.

00:29:55.556 --> 00:30:00.000
First, a search relevance workbench,
a systematic framework for evaluating and tuning search relevance.

